+# Best AI Tools for Restaurants 2026
+
+[![Awesome](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/sindresorhus/awesome@main/media/badge.svg)](https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome)
+[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
+![Topics](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/GagnDeep/awesome-best-ai-tools-for-restaurants-2026?style=social)
+
+> Best AI tools for restaurants 2026 is a curated list of open source GitHub repositories that restaurants can actually self-host or adapt for guest messaging, voice ordering, menu OCR, forecasting, and internal automation. Direct restaurant-specific open source AI repos are still rare, so this list broadens into adjacent production-grade tools that map cleanly to restaurant workflows.
+
+## Table of Contents
+- [TL;DR](#tldr)
+- [Why This List](#why-this-list)
+- [Open Source Tools](#open-source-tools)
+- [FAQ](#faq)
+- [Contributing](#contributing)
+- [License](#license)
+
+## TL;DR
+- `RasaHQ/rasa`, `botpress/botpress`, and `theopenconversationkit/tock` are the strongest open source foundations for restaurant chat, reservations, FAQs, and guest-service automations.
+- `pipecat-ai/pipecat`, `vocodedev/vocode-core`, `openai/whisper`, `coqui-ai/TTS`, and `rhasspy/piper` are the most practical starting stack for voice ordering and phone automation.
+- `JaidedAI/EasyOCR`, `Unstructured-IO/unstructured`, `Layout-Parser/layout-parser`, and `run-llama/liteparse` are the clearest fits for menu ingestion, invoice parsing, and document-heavy back office workflows.
+- `Nixtla/neuralforecast`, `unit8co/darts`, `sktime/pytorch-forecasting`, and `Nixtla/mlforecast` are the best open source choices for demand forecasting, prep planning, and labor scheduling models.
+- There is still a major open source gap in restaurant-native POS copilot, table management AI, and kitchen AI products; where that gap exists, this README says so directly.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source matters for restaurants because margins are tight, workflows are operationally messy, and vendor lock-in is expensive. The best AI tools for restaurants 2026 are not only about flashy chat interfaces; they are about owning your data, self-hosting where needed, integrating with existing ordering or POS systems, and adapting models to local menus, hours, languages, and operational constraints. This list only includes public GitHub repositories and excludes proprietary SaaS-only products.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Guest Messaging, Reservations, and Conversational AI
+
+#### [Rasa](https://github.com/RasaHQ/rasa)
+> **Description:** Rasa is an open source conversational AI framework for building reliable text and voice agents with business logic, memory, and multi-turn dialogue control. Its README positions it as a foundation for production assistants rather than a demo chatbot, which makes it a strong fit for restaurant reservation flows, FAQ automation, loyalty questions, and handoff to staff. GitHub shows it as Apache-2.0 licensed, primarily Python, with 20,961 stars and a last commit date of Dec 18, 2025. For restaurants, the main advantage is control over intents, policies, forms, and integrations instead of depending on a hosted black box.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/RasaHQ/rasa](https://github.com/RasaHQ/rasa)
+- **Stars:** 20,961 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-12-18
+- **Category:** chatbot, conversational-ai, reservations
+- **Best for:** Structured guest support bots, reservation assistants, and policy-heavy service workflows
+
+---
+
+#### [Botpress](https://github.com/botpress/botpress)
+> **Description:** Botpress describes its repository as the open source hub to build and deploy GPT and LLM agents, with integrations, devtools, bots-as-code examples, and plugin support. For restaurants, that is useful when you need a web or messaging assistant that can answer menu questions, capture leads for catering, route customer issues, or trigger external systems. GitHub lists the repo as MIT licensed, primarily TypeScript, with 14,481 stars and a last commit date of Jan 10, 2026. The project is especially practical for teams that want a modern agent workflow without committing to a proprietary chatbot builder.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/botpress/botpress](https://github.com/botpress/botpress)
+- **Stars:** 14,481 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-10
+- **Category:** chatbot, llm-agents, customer-service
+- **Best for:** Fast deployment of restaurant support bots and lead-capture flows
+
+---
+
+#### [Tiledesk](https://github.com/Tiledesk/tiledesk)
+> **Description:** Tiledesk’s README frames the project as an open source agentic AI operating system with multichannel support, humans-in-the-loop, native RAG, APIs, and deployment via Docker Compose or Kubernetes. That makes it relevant for restaurants that want one system for web chat, WhatsApp, SMS, or support inbox automation without buying a closed platform. GitHub lists the repository as MIT licensed, with Mustache as the primary language, 259 stars, and a last commit date of Nov 19, 2025. Its self-hosted packaging focus is the main differentiator for operators who care about infrastructure ownership and customization.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/Tiledesk/tiledesk](https://github.com/Tiledesk/tiledesk)
+- **Stars:** 259 ⭐
+- **Language:** Mustache
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2025-11-19
+- **Category:** self-hosted, multichannel, support
+- **Best for:** Self-hosted restaurant support stacks spanning website chat and messaging channels
+
